Command:     CREATE KEK
Description: define a TECC key-encryption key
Syntax:
CREATE KEK [ IF NOT EXISTS ] kek_name
    WITH ( option = value [, ...] )

URL: https://www.postgresql.org/docs/14/sql-createkek.html

Command:     ALTER KEK
Description: rotate a TECC key-encryption key
Syntax:
ALTER KEK kek_name
    WITH ( KEY_PATH = value )

URL: https://www.postgresql.org/docs/14/sql-alterkek.html

Command:     CREATE DEK
Description: define a TECC data-encryption key
Syntax:
CREATE DEK [ IF NOT EXISTS ] dek_name
    WITH VALUES ( KEK = kek_name [, option = value ...] )

URL: https://www.postgresql.org/docs/14/sql-createdek.html

Command:     DROP KEK
Description: remove a TECC key-encryption key
Syntax:
DROP KEK [ IF EXISTS ] kek_name [, ...] [ CASCADE | RESTRICT ]

URL: https://www.postgresql.org/docs/14/sql-dropkek.html

Command:     DROP DEK
Description: remove a TECC data-encryption key
Syntax:
DROP DEK [ IF EXISTS ] dek_name [, ...] [ CASCADE | RESTRICT ]

URL: https://www.postgresql.org/docs/14/sql-dropdek.html

TECC
  Connection parameter: enable_tecc=true|false (env: PGENABLETECC, default: true)
  psql option:          -k on|off controls TECC connection preprocessing
  Session mode:         \enable hg_tecc.mode / \disable hg_tecc.mode / \show hg_tecc.mode
  Syntax help:          \h CREATE KEK, \h ALTER KEK, \h CREATE DEK, \h DROP KEK, \h DROP DEK
  Show DDL:             \sT table_name, \sK kek_name, \sk dek_name
  Show TECC objects:    \dkek[+] [PATTERN], \ddek[+] [PATTERN]
  Column clause:        ENCRYPTED WITH (DEK = dek_name [, SUPPORT_ALGO = 'DET|OPE|SEARCH'])

Formatting
